\paragraph{Convergent nocuity and multi-agent safety.}
The 41.3\% convergent violation rate challenges multi-agent agreement as a safety proxy: cross-family violation co-occurrence (Jaccard $= 0.46$; Figure~\ref{fig:convergent_nocuity}b) approaches within-family levels ($0.54$). Unlike sycophancy, which requires exposure to a preferred answer, convergent nocuity arises from shared structural biases---the 77\% permissive attractor operates without user pressure or inter-model communication---so adding redundant agents will not catch these failures. Effective monitoring requires adversarial designs that test for convergent misinterpretation rather than inter-model disagreement.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
Developers should conduct clause-level ambiguity audits before deployment rather than type-level screening (Appendix~\ref{app:detection}). The type--mechanism dissociation enables a three-stage workflow: (1)~flag clauses containing any of the six types, prioritizing incompleteness and authorization scope; (2)~use the type--mechanism mapping to predict failure modes and design targeted test cases; (3)~apply minimal disambiguating edits following the matched-pair methodology. The matched-pair design itself constitutes a reusable methodology: practitioners can construct clause variants and measure the violation delta as a clause-level risk score.

